 are collecting data um on that  interaction  so we at the end of an experiment we  have  a bucket of drops we can arrange those  drops  based on what the experiment did into  these interactions  which are usually experiment specific  sometimes if we're looking at qc  we'll want to be looking at a particular  drop in every sequence let's say we  really want to look at baseline  over the course of an experiment we can  look at all the buffer drops every time  buffer was in contact  historically over an experiment with a  sensor  we can group all those together and very  easily look at those  without having to you know look at other  things that may not be  related to it but if we're looking at  interactions for sure we're looking at  these kind of like baseline drops as you  can see on the right  the solution sample and then usually  that dissociation phase  where we're looking at the decay and the  interaction we can group  everything together based exactly on  what we need  and then perform further analyses from  there  everything that we do in the  analysis tools is kind of best effort at  first approach so  what this means is as a user when you  log into the portal  if an experiment is complete your  results are immediately available  we of course provide all of these  different views that you see here to  allow you to go in and tweak data  and continue to explore it and  you know re-analyze it as necessary but  we also  do the best we can to design the  software  to direct you to anything that needs  your attention right so you can filter  and group by lots of different tags  um the good data obviously you  probably don't need to interact with but  certainly if there's problematic data  that you might want to try to salvage or  use in some way  that the software is is unable to or you  know recommends against  you still have the freedom to do that um  all of this is built upon a  classification and tagging system  this is super exciting um so if you are  not  into data science you probably work with  the data science group i'm sure  um and certainly they will  uh have a lot of things to say about  this but um we basically  um have multiple tags available for  every kind of experimental feature those  are the interactions themselves  the samples things that you know are you  care about  um from the experiment perspective as  well as system features so sensors  again let's say a sensor gets fouled  that's something that you might want to  know but is based on  the sensor itself then right and not the  actual interactions  um and then wells drop paths things like  that  um and this is kind of an example you  can see on the left here  um a couple simple tags um this is a  regeneration qc  it looks like and then on over here  we've got some kinetic analyses  and a lot of different classifications  as well for uh biphasic interactions  um non-binding any anything that we can  help you identify we will do  and you can of course overwrite and  redefine the classifications as needed  um in the same set of tools  um really briefly today just wanted to  introduce a couple simple things around  some application specific modules um  just to let you know what's coming  um as we release more and more tools i  think that's one of the the coolest  things about this  web-based analysis package is it makes  it very easy for us to continue to push  new features  to our users and help them get more out  of the machines  so these are a couple examples of some  epitope characterization  tools that we have coming out um  and a lot of you know we have  purpose-built data visualizations  and you know again tools that to try to  help you get  the most out of your sensograms and  finally  the last point to note on how we're  automating analysis is that  all of the tools that we make available  um to support the web app are also  available on device  um so it's an npm package that probably  doesn't mean anything to  most people here but um we it can run on  the device it runs in the cloud it can  run anywhere our data goes  basically so on the device especially in  real time we can analyze data  and make help the device makes decisions  in this case the device is telling us  that  looks like an affinity is likely single  digit nanomolar that's great um  the off rate looks pretty fast and we  have a one micromolar stock in the  cartridge  so the device in this case can make a  decision  to start off at 300 nanomolar and  perform a 3x resolution  over five points and that should  perfectly bracket the kd based on that  input information which is a lot of the  information that you might have done  in a lengthy kind of optimization  process  and then over here on the right uh we've  got our  a little some notifications saying that  analysis is available because an  experiment completed on the device  um as soon as it's done on the device  you know that in the cloud and it looks  like  um there are a couple interactions that  need review and it's letting us know  that as well  finally automating results getting data  where it needs to go  i don't have a lot to share at this  stage but all of this  is being built into our nokoya apis we  have  planned out a rest api and data services  that can help our users  get data where it needs to go let's say  you're you know  you've built out an automated uh  characterization pipeline and you need  whenever an experiment finishes you want  to set up a trigger  to go and fetch that data or just the  interactions  objects or whatever you need from that  experiment to feed your data pipeline  these apis will help you do that  securely and  get it get your data wherever it needs  to go  as well as set up experiments and a  couple other cool features that we'll  talk about in a future presentation  so welcome to the ecosystem this is a  digitally transformed platform for  discovery  with optical biosensor data  that's awesome thank you adam and thanks  so much everyone for joining us today  um this concludes our overview of an  ecosystem and the effect  that digital microfluidics brings to  optical biosensors  for those of you who find yourself  studying biomolecular interactions and  are looking to transform your journey  with label-free biosensors  we invite you to join our growing base  of ecosystem users  for any questions about today's  presentation you can reach us via the  contact information that's on this slide  and we really look forward to hearing  from you thanks so much everyone  have a great rest of your day
 

